---
import { LINKS, SITE_UPDATED, STORAGE } from '@lib/shared/constants';
import { formatDateStamp } from '@lib/shared/utils';

type Inline =
    | { attributes: Record<string, string>; kind: 'link'; label: string }
    | { code: string; kind: 'code' }
    | { kind: 'text'; text: string };

interface Props {
    kind: 'privacy' | 'terms';
}

const ATTRIBUTE_PATTERN = /([a-z]+)="([^"]*)"/g;
const INLINE_PATTERN = /<a ([^>]+)>(.*?)<\/a>|<code>(.*?)<\/code>/g;

const SECTIONS: Record<Props['kind'], { body: string[]; heading: string }[]> = {
    privacy: [
        {
            body: [
                'Accounts, and what members do with them, live with Supabase, the database service this site runs on. An account holds your email address, a hash of your password (never the password itself), the profile you fill in (artist name, portrait, bio, city and state, genres, role, links, and a Spotlight track if you upload one), the events you check in to, and your Prods ledger. Your portrait is stored as an image anyone with its link can open. Once you finish onboarding, your card is public: the directory shows your portrait, artist name, role, genres, city and state, bio, links, Spotlight track, and when you joined, and anyone can play the Spotlight track there; the content API returns the same details and your member number, without the portrait or the audio. The rest of your account is visible only to you and the organizers, apart from the artist name shown on the messages and board entries you post. Chat messages are stored with your artist name and the time, are visible to signed-in members, and are deleted with your account. A link you queue or a file you upload to an Assembly or Bash board is stored with your artist name and listed to signed-in members; audio uploaded to a board can be played only by the organizers, you can remove your own entries, and the organizers clear the board after the event. There is no button to delete an account: write to us and the organizers delete it, along with your profile and card, Prods ledger, attached check-ins, chat messages, and board entries; orders and any newsletter subscription stay on file by email address.',
                'The specifics people reasonably worry about. Checking in at the door stores the address you enter, the event, and the time. Until you are signed in with that address, the check-in stays a bare record that adds nothing to any account; it is attached to your account, along with its Prod, the first time you are. When the address has no confirmed account yet, checking in also asks Supabase to email it a sign-in link. The contact form sends your name, email, topic, and message to the site\'s server, which relays them by email through Resend; they are not stored in the database. The newsletter stores your address, where you gave it (the home page or the door), and the dates you subscribed and unsubscribed. The welcome email it sends carries an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing keeps the address on file as unsubscribed; only the signed-in owner of that address can put it back on the list. Checkout sends your card details straight to Stripe, our payment processor; they never reach our servers and we never store them. Stripe also receives the name, email, and shipping address you enter, along with the order number and amount. An order keeps its number, the line items, the totals, a timestamp, the email and shipping address you entered, and your account when you are signed in. The AI assistant sends each question to the site\'s server and, once it is configured, on to Supabase to search the site and to Anthropic to write the answer; questions are not stored. Files you load into the rack are read on your device and sent nowhere.',
            ],
            heading: 'What we collect',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'A few features remember things between pages or visits, using your browser\'s built-in storage: seven keys in all, every one of them in localStorage on your device.',
                `Three of them come with signing in: <code>${STORAGE.session.key}</code>, a cached copy of your account (profile, email, join date, onboarding state, and Prods balance), so pages can show it before Supabase answers; Supabase's own sign-in entry, whose key starts with <code>sb-</code> and ends in <code>-auth-token</code>, holding the tokens that keep you signed in and the user record Supabase returned, which the site sends with each request it makes for you to Supabase or to its own server; and <code>${STORAGE.onboardingDraft.key}</code>, the onboarding step you reached and the account it belongs to.`,
                `The remaining four sit alongside them, and keep what they hold just the same: <code>${STORAGE.cart.key}</code>, your cart; <code>${STORAGE.order.key}</code>, the receipt from your most recent order; <code>${STORAGE.theme.key}</code>, your theme choice; and <code>${STORAGE.tour.key}</code>, a flag set when you choose the guided tour at the end of onboarding and cleared when the home page picks it up to start the tour.`,
                'The rack, the track player, the member chat, the submission boards, and the AI assistant keep their state in page memory only, so leaving the page ends it; chat messages and board entries themselves stay with Supabase. Every key sits there until you clear it or the site clears it for you. Placing an order empties the cart on the spot. Signing out removes your cached account and Supabase\'s sign-in entry and leaves the rest in place. Clearing site data for this domain removes all seven keys at once and signs you out on that browser; your account stays with Supabase.',
            ],
            heading: 'Data stored in your browser',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'This site sets no cookies and runs no analytics: no tracking pixels, no ad networks, and no third-party scripts, fonts, or frames, with one exception. Once payments are configured, the checkout page loads Stripe\'s script and payment frames from Stripe\'s own domain, and Stripe may set its own cookies there for fraud prevention. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control change nothing here because there is nothing to switch off; consider the signal honored on arrival.',
            ],
            heading: 'Cookies & tracking',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'The site is served by Netlify, which keeps standard server access logs (IP address, requested page, timestamp, browser) as part of operating its network. Those logs live with Netlify, not with us, and its <a href="https://netlify.com/privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">privacy policy</a> covers them. Netlify also holds the request counters the site uses to rate-limit its server routes and file downloads, most of them keyed by IP address. Supabase also logs each sign-in and other account activity, such as a password change, with the time and the IP address it came from, and the organizers can read that log. The other services that handle your information are the ones named above: Supabase (accounts and their sign-in emails, check-ins, chat, the boards, the newsletter list, orders, and the assistant\'s search), Stripe (card payments), Resend (contact, newsletter, and account welcome email), and Anthropic (the assistant\'s answers, once it is configured).',
                `The Alliance also organizes on <a href="${LINKS.instagram}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Instagram</a>, <a href="${LINKS.meetup}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Meetup</a>, <a href="${LINKS.discord}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Discord</a>, <a href="${LINKS.spotify}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Spotify</a>, <a href="${LINKS.soundcloud}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SoundCloud</a>, and <a href="${LINKS.linktree}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Linktree</a>. Monthly support and donations on the partners page go through Patreon. Following those links takes you off this site and onto each service's own terms and privacy practices.`,
            ],
            heading: 'Hosting & third parties',
        },
        {
            body: [
                `Write to <a href="mailto:${LINKS.email}">${LINKS.email}</a> and we receive your address and whatever you chose to say. We use them to reply and for nothing else: no lists, no sharing, no automated processing. Mail sits in an ordinary mailbox; ask us to delete a thread and we delete it.`,
            ],
            heading: 'Email',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'This site is not directed at children under 13. Membership starts at the same age. If you believe a child has emailed us personal information, write to us and we delete it.',
            ],
            heading: 'Children',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'This policy describes the site as built. The date at the top marks the current version.',
            ],
            heading: 'Changes to this policy',
        },
    ],
    terms: [
        {
            body: [
                'You need to be 13 or older to use this site, and membership starts at the same age. Venues set event ages: where a room is 18+ or 21+, the venue\'s rule holds over anything written here, so read the listing before you plan around it.',
            ],
            heading: 'Eligibility',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'Membership is open to anyone making music in or around Austin. No dues, no auditions; it is personal and non-transferable. One standing rule travels with it: treat the room the way you want the scene to be. Harassment, discrimination, and gatekeeping have no home here, in person or in the Discord.',
                'Leaving is simple: stop coming and leave the Discord, and you are no longer a member. Your account on this site is kept with Supabase, and the privacy policy lists what it holds and how to have it deleted. The other direction belongs to the organizers, who may warn, turn away, or remove anyone who makes the community worse, with no notice when the safety of the room is the reason, and whose call is final. Removal changes nothing about what you own: your tracks, samples, and name stay yours.',
            ],
            heading: 'Membership & conduct',
        },
        {
            body: [
                `Events happen in rooms our venue sponsors lend us, and attending means following the house rules of whichever space we are in. RSVPs run through <a href="${LINKS.meetup}" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Meetup</a> and some rooms cap attendance. An RSVP is a plan, not a contract, but tell us if yours changes.`,
                'Most events are photographed and some are filmed for recaps, and attending means consenting to that coverage. Tell an organizer at check-in and we keep you out of frame; email us and a published photo of you comes down.',
            ],
            heading: 'Events & photography',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'Feedback at an Assembly is opinion, offered producer to producer, not professional, legal, or A&R advice. Playing a work-in-progress for the room is not publication and gives nobody in it any rights, and recording someone else\'s unreleased work without asking is a conduct violation.',
                'Submitting to the <a href="/bashes">Bash</a> transfers no ownership. You grant only what the event needs: permission to play submissions at the Bash, to feature them in recaps, and to include them, with credit, in a community compilation. Submit only material you have the rights to share. Uncleared work comes down, and the clearance stays your responsibility.',
                'Links and files you submit to the boards, and messages you post in the member chat, are stored with Supabase and listed to signed-in members under your artist name; audio uploaded to a board can be played only by the organizers. You can remove your own board entries, and the organizers clear each board after its event. Once you finish onboarding, your card is public on the directory and through the content API, and anyone can play its Spotlight track.',
            ],
            heading: 'Your work',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'The store takes card payments through Stripe at checkout, with 8.25% sales tax added to each order. Refunds go back through Stripe to the card that paid; write to us to ask for one, and a full refund also returns any Prods the order redeemed. Your cart lives in your browser, and the privacy policy lists what an order keeps.',
                'Prods are the Alliance\'s participation currency: members earn them for showing up and contributing, and each is worth $1.00 of credit toward any item in the store, applied at checkout. Prods are not money: they have no cash value and cannot be bought, sold, or transferred, and the program can change or wind down as the Alliance evolves.',
            ],
            heading: 'Store & Prods',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'Use the site as a visitor, not a target. Do not probe or disrupt it, harvest it for spam lists, or impersonate the Alliance or its members. The <a href="/rack">rack</a> does real work in your browser. Load only audio you have the right to load, and watch your levels.',
            ],
            heading: 'Acceptable use',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'The Alliance name, the symbol, and the writing and design of this site belong to the Austin Producer Alliance. Press and partners are welcome to the brand assets in our <a href="/resources">resource library</a>; ask before reusing anything else. Producer work shown here belongs to the producers who made it, and nothing on this site grants you a license to any of it.',
            ],
            heading: 'Intellectual property',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'This site and everything the Alliance runs are provided as-is by a volunteer community organization, without warranties of any kind. To the fullest extent the law allows, the Alliance and its organizers are not liable for indirect or consequential damages arising from the site or attendance at events; nothing here limits liability that cannot lawfully be limited.',
                `Texas law governs these terms, and disputes belong in the state or federal courts of Travis County. If a court strikes part of these terms, the rest stands. These terms and the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> are the entire agreement between you and the Alliance about this site and its events, and a term we do not enforce today stays enforceable tomorrow. Before any of that, write to <a href="mailto:${LINKS.email}">${LINKS.email}</a>. A volunteer organization and a filing fee are a poor match.`,
            ],
            heading: 'Liability & law',
        },
        {
            body: [
                'These terms cover the site and the events as they run today. The date at the top marks the current version, and continuing to use the site or attend events after a change means the new terms apply.',
            ],
            heading: 'Changes to these terms',
        },
    ],
};

const { kind } = Astro.props;
const updated = new Date(SITE_UPDATED);

const crossLink = kind === 'privacy'
    ? { href: '/terms', label: 'terms of service' }
    : { href: '/privacy', label: 'privacy policy' };

const title = kind === 'privacy' ? 'Privacy policy' : 'Terms of service';

function parseAttributes(source: string) {
    return Object.fromEntries(Array.from(source.matchAll(ATTRIBUTE_PATTERN), ([, name, value]) => [name, value]));
}

function parseInline(html: string) {
    const segments: Inline[] = [];

    let cursor = 0;

    for (const match of html.matchAll(INLINE_PATTERN)) {
        const [whole, attributes, label, code] = match;

        if (match.index > cursor) segments.push({ kind: 'text', text: html.slice(cursor, match.index) });

        segments.push(code === undefined ? { attributes: parseAttributes(attributes), kind: 'link', label } : { code, kind: 'code' });
        cursor = match.index + whole.length;
    }

    if (cursor < html.length) segments.push({ kind: 'text', text: html.slice(cursor) });

    return segments;
}
---

<section
    class="stage stage--tight"
    aria-labelledby={`${kind}-title`}
>
    <div>
        <p
            class="label mb-4 text-link"
            data-motion="stamp"
        >
            Last updated <time datetime={SITE_UPDATED}>{formatDateStamp(updated)}</time>
        </p>
        <h1
            id={`${kind}-title`}
            class="mb-10 text-4xl-7xl"
            data-motion="strike"
        >
            {title}
        </h1>
        <div
            class="legal__body space-y-6 text-base"
            data-motion="rise"
        >
            {SECTIONS[kind].map(section => (
                <>
                    <h2 class="first:pt-0 pt-4 text-2xl text-balance">
                        {section.heading}
                    </h2>
                    {section.body.map(paragraph => (
                        <p>
                            {parseInline(paragraph).map((segment) => {
                                if (segment.kind === 'code') return <code class="px-1 border border-border-subtle whitespace-nowrap bg-code-bg">{segment.code}</code>;
                                if (segment.kind === 'text') return segment.text;

                                return <a {...segment.attributes}>{segment.label}</a>;
                            })}
                        </p>
                    ))}
                </>
            ))}
            <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl text-balance">
                Contact
            </h2>
            <p>
                Email <a href={`mailto:${LINKS.email}`}>{LINKS.email}</a> with any questions about this document. It pairs with our <a href={crossLink.href}>{crossLink.label}</a>, and together the two are the whole of the paperwork.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<style>
    .legal__body a {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        position: relative;
        text-decoration-line: underline;
        text-underline-offset: 4px;
        top: 0;
        transition:
            color var(--duration-fast) var(--motion-ease-ui),
            top var(--duration-fast) var(--motion-ease-ui);
    }

    .legal__body a:active {
        top: 1px;
    }

    .legal__body a:hover {
        color: var(--color-link);
    }
</style>
